Subject: WebKit on Phoenix: the UI process watches the system's free memory and sends memory pressure events

On Linux the UI process runs a MemoryPressureMonitor thread (UIProcess/linux/): it reads
/proc/meminfo every 1-5 s and, with 90 % of the memory used, sends each web and network process a
memory pressure event (AuxiliaryProcess::DidReceiveMemoryPressureEvent; critical at 95 %), on
which they release their caches. On Phoenix all of it was compiled out (OS(LINUX)), so no process
ever heard that the system was running out: the only trigger left was each web process's own
footprint (patch 0024's periodic monitor, 1 GB / 1.5 GB of a 3 GB limit), blind to the other
processes. A YouTube session (C17) then took the system to 0 KB free, every allocation failed at
once, and the desktop was left unusable.

On Phoenix the monitor reads the kernel's page counters with meminfo() (the panel's memory plugin
and sysconf(_SC_AVPHYS_PAGES) do the same; free includes the file cache the kernel gives up on
demand) and keeps the Linux plumbing as it is:

  warning   free below 20 % of RAM                     non-critical event
  critical  free below 10 % of RAM or below 300 MB     critical event

polled every 5 s with less than half the memory in use, down to every 1 s above 85 % (Linux's
intervals). Each event also runs the UI process's own handler (installed on Phoenix as on Linux):
it empties the back/forward and web process caches, shuts prewarmed web processes down and drops
GTK's view snapshots -- whole processes exiting, the one release that returns memory to the
system, as mimalloc does not decommit on Phoenix (patch 0005). WEBKIT_DISABLE_MEMORY_PRESSURE_MONITOR=1
turns it all off, as on Linux. Each change of level is printed on stderr, next to 0024's lines:

  WPEB-MEMPRESSURE pid=P system level=normal|warning|critical free_mb=F total_mb=T

